Experiment 7: Packet Analysis of TCP & UDP Protocols Objectives Capture, inspect, and compare Transport Layer protocols (TCP and UDP) using Wireshark. Protocol Comparison Feature TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) UDP (User Datagram Protocol) Connection Type Connection-Oriented (3-Way Handshake) Connectionless Header Size 20 to 60 Bytes (Variable) 8 Bytes (Fixed) Reliability Guaranteed delivery via ACKs & Retransmissions Best-effort delivery Header Fields Src/Dst Port, Seq No, Ack No, Window, Flags Src/Dst Port, Length, Checksum Wireshark Filters tcp : Displays TCP segment traffic showing 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK). udp : Displays UDP datagram traffic showing lightweight single-packet DNS/media requests. Practical Examination Write-Up: Experiment 7

AIM To capture, inspect, and comparatively analyze Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and User Datagram Protocol (UDP) packets at the Transport Layer using Wireshark Packet Analyzer.

THEORY Transport Layer Role: Provides logical process-to-process communication between application processes running on different hosts. Uses 16-bit port numbers (0 to 65535) for multiplexing and demultiplexing. TCP Protocol Structure & Mechanisms: Header Size: 20 to 60 Bytes (variable length depending on Options field). Control Flags (6 bits minimum): URG, ACK, PSH, RST, SYN, FIN. Connection Establishment (3-Way Handshake): Client $ ightarrow$ Server: [SYN], Seq = Initial Seq Number ( I S N c ). Server $ ightarrow$ Client: [SYN, ACK], Seq = I S N s , Ack = I S N c

1 . Client $ ightarrow$ Server: [ACK], Seq = I S N c + 1 , Ack = I S N s + 1 . Reliability: Maintained through Sequence Numbers, Acknowledgments, Retransmission Timers (RTO), and Checksum. Flow Control: Sliding Window mechanism via Advertised Window Size field. UDP Protocol Structure & Mechanisms: Header Size: Fixed 8 Bytes (minimal protocol overhead). Fields: Source Port (16-bit), Destination Port (16-bit), Length (16-bit), Checksum (16-bit). Operation: Connectionless, best-effort datagram service without ordering, acknowledgment, flow control, or congestion control. 3. EXPLANATION / PROCEDURE Packet Capture Setup: Start capture in Wireshark on active network interface. TCP Analysis: In browser/terminal, access an HTTP/HTTPS website to initiate TCP traffic. Apply Wireshark filter: tcp.flags.syn == 1. Locate and examine the 3-Way Handshake sequence (SYN $ ightarrow$ SYN, ACK $ ightarrow$ ACK). Inspect Sequence Numbers (relative vs. raw), Acknowledgment Numbers, TCP Header Length (Data Offset), Window Size scaling, and MSS (Maximum Segment Size) in TCP Options. Inspect connection termination: locate FIN and ACK segments. UDP Analysis: Filter Wireshark capture with udp. Inspect a DNS or NTP datagram: note the compact 8-byte header structure without connection flags, sequence numbers, or window sizes. Comparative Analysis: Compare overhead, handshake latency, and packet header complexity between TCP and UDP captures. 4. CONCLUSION Successfully inspected and compared Transport Layer protocols using Wireshark. Confirmed that TCP provides reliable, ordered data delivery at the expense of header overhead (20–60 bytes) and 3-way handshake latency, while UDP provides minimal header overhead (8 bytes) and low latency without delivery guarantees. -->
